{"name":"Larb Gai","hex":"#dfc6aa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"larb","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Laotian or Thai spicy salad with ground, chopped, or minced meat and vegetables."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/larb"},{"term":"gai","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Borei."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gai"}],"entries":[{"title":"Larb","blocks":[{"text":"Laab / Larb is a type of Lao meat salad that is the national dish of Laos, along with green papaya salad and sticky rice. Laab in the Lao language is a noun that refers to meat or other flesh that has been finely chopped and pounded. It is also considered a food of good luck in both Laos and Thailand because it has homonyms that mean 'lucky' in both languages, derived from लाभ in Sanskrit. Laab is of Lao origin, but is also eaten in other regions, most prominently the neighboring former Lan Xang territory, or modern day Laos and the northeastern and northern areas of Thailand, Isan and Lanna where the Lao have extended their influence. Other local variants of laab also feature in the cuisines of the Tai peoples of Shan State, Burma, and Yunnan Province, China."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/LaoFood_LarbNeua.JPG/330px-LaoFood_LarbNeua.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larb","wikidata":"Q3052288"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lao_meat_Larb.jpg/500px-Lao_meat_Larb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lao_meat_Larb.jpg/1280px-Lao_meat_Larb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lao_meat_Larb.jpg/500px-Lao_meat_Larb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lao bile laab (laab Pia in Lao) made with cooked meat, tripes, and bile","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALao_meat_Larb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Lao_beef_nam_tok_%28Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok%29.jpg/500px-Lao_beef_nam_tok_%28Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Lao_beef_nam_tok_%28Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok%29.jpg/1280px-Lao_beef_nam_tok_%28Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Lao_beef_nam_tok_%28Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok%29.jpg/500px-Lao_beef_nam_tok_%28Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ping sin nam tok is a variation of Lao laab dish made with beef steak and sometimes enjoyed as an appetizer with aperitif.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALao_beef_nam_tok_(Lao_ping_sin_nam_tok).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Lao_fish_Larb_%28Goi_Paa%29.jpg/500px-Lao_fish_Larb_%28Goi_Paa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Lao_fish_Larb_%28Goi_Paa%29.jpg/1280px-Lao_fish_Larb_%28Goi_Paa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Lao_fish_Larb_%28Goi_Paa%29.jpg/500px-Lao_fish_Larb_%28Goi_Paa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lao Goi made with Mekong fish served with sticky rice and plenty of fresh vegetables","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALao_fish_Larb_(Goi_Paa).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Lao_Sua_